%[text] # Slicer Femur Analysis
%[text] In this live script, we load a Slicer segmentation of the legs and measure the femur. First, we align the femur's long axis with the z-axis and measure its length, which we use to estimate the person's height. Then we cut the femur into thin cross-sections along its long axis, and use them to find the shaft and measure its diameter.
%[text] ### Learning Objectives
%[text] - Calculate the volume and mean Hounsfield units of each segment
%[text] - Align a surface to the cardinal axes, and measure the length of a bone
%[text] - Estimate a person's height from the length of the femur
%[text] - Measure the cross-sectional area and diameter of a bone along its long axis
%[text] - Explain why different methods give different values for the "diameter" of a bone \
%[text] ### Before you start
%[text] - Segment the femurs in Slicer, and measure their length and shaft diameter with the Slicer ruler (Markups → Line)
%[text] - You can use your own Slicer files, or the example files in the Femur folder of the unit 3 data folder \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## Load Your Data
%[text] Choose your own Femur folder with the folder browser. If you don't have your own files, use the optional code below to load the course example files.
close all hidden
clearvars

paths.project = "/Users/ernesto/MATLAB-Drive/MtMresources/data/unit3/Femur"; % choose your own Femur folder %[control:filebrowser:5635]{"position":[17,76]}
% display folder contents
contents = dir(fullfile(paths.project,"*.nrrd")); % find the slicer volume files in folder
disp((1:numel(contents))' + ". " + string({contents.name})') % display folder content info %[output:6e26bed7]
%%
%[text] Enter the numbers for the Intensity Volume, Segmentation Volume.
paths.intensity = fullfile(paths.project, contents(1).name); % Number of the CT volume %[control:spinner:4610]{"position":[52,53]}
paths.segment = fullfile(paths.project, contents(2).name) % Number of your Segmentation volume %[control:spinner:3201]{"position":[50,51]} %[output:2734485f]
%%
%[text] ### OPTIONAL: No data, no problem
%[text] Run this code block to get the course example (uncomment the line of code first)
%paths = getExamplePath %[output:60d7c120]
function paths = getExamplePath
unit3f = mmSetUnitDataFolder(3); % change Current Folder to the Unit 3 Data Folder and get path
paths.project = fullfile(unit3f, "Femur"); % example files (or the path to your own Femur folder)
paths.intensity = fullfile(paths.project, "6 BODY BONE cropped.nrrd");
paths.segment = fullfile(paths.project, "Salcedo Segmentation.seg.nrrd");
end
%%
%[text] ### Check your work
%[text] Make sure files exist:
table(fieldnames(paths), structfun(@(x) exist(x, "file"), paths), VariableNames=["path" "X"]) %[output:0b3dc949]
%[text] - 7 means folder, 2 means file
%[text] - A zero in the X column means that the file doesn't exist. Check the file names \
%%
%[text] ### Load the volumes as medical volumes
%[text] A **medicalVolume** holds the voxels together with their metadata (voxel spacing, orientation, and the transformation to mm).
warning("off", "medical:medicalVolume:noEndian") % Slicer's NRRD files trigger a harmless warning
ctMV = medicalVolume(paths.intensity) % CT volume %[output:18ac982d]
segMV = medicalVolume(paths.segment) % segmentation volume %[output:10df9755]
%[text] - The voxels are cubic: about 1.07 mm on each side
%[text] - Both volumes have the same size, so each voxel of the segmentation matches a voxel of the CT \
%%
%[text] ### Load the segment information
%[text] The segment names, labels, and colors that you set in Slicer are saved in the header of the segmentation file. The course function **mmGetSlicerSegmentInfo** reads them into a table:
segT = mmGetSlicerSegmentInfo(paths.segment) %[output:0eb9368b]
%[text] - This segmentation has six segments, with the label values 1-6 \
%%
%[text] ## Plot all segmentations
%[text] The course function **mmPlotAllSeg** plots every segment in its Slicer color. Its inputs are the segmentation volume and the segment table.
mmPlotAllSeg(segMV, segT, smooth=true, new_figure=true); %[output:492ef9d3]
%[text] - This segmentation includes the tibias, which we didn't segment in class
%[text] - The left knee has an implant (a knee replacement). Notice how the implant replaces the condyles of the left femur and the top of the left tibia \
%%
%[text] ## Volume and mean Hounsfield units
%[text] **regionprops3** can measure every segment at once, if we label the voxels with a categorical array. With the CT volume as the second input, it also calculates the mean intensity (in Hounsfield units, HU) of each segment.
segCat = categorical(segMV.Voxels, segT.LabelValue, segT.SegName); % label each voxel by its segment name
rp = regionprops3(segCat, ctMV.Voxels, "Volume", "MeanIntensity");
rp.VolumeCM3 = rp.Volume * prod(0.1*segMV.VoxelSpacing) % convert voxels to cm^3 %[output:7c44b27c]
%[text] - We convert the voxel spacing to cm before we multiply, because bone volumes are usually reported in cm^3 (1 cm^3 = 1 mL)
%[text] - Bone density is correlated with Hounsfield units, so the mean HU tells us how dense each bone is
%[text] - The implants are metal, so their mean HU is much higher than any bone \
%%
%[text] # Femur length
%[text] Femur length is about 26% of a person's height, so it's often used to estimate height. Anthropologists measure femur length on an osteometric board (a bone box), with the femur lying flat:
%[text] ![](text:image:5f58)
%[text] To do the same in MATLAB, we first align the long axis of the femur with one of the cardinal axes. Then the length of the femur is just the range of its vertices along that axis.
%%
%[text] ## Index out the right femur
%[text] The right femur doesn't have an implant, so we start with that one. The course function **mmGetMedicalVolumeSegment** finds a segment by name, and returns a structure with:
%[text] - **mask**: a logical volume, true for every voxel of the segment
%[text] - **color**: the Slicer color of the segment
%[text] - **tform**: the transformation from voxels to mm
%[text] - **spacing**: the voxel spacing
%[text] - **volume**: the volume of the segment, in voxels \
rSeg = mmGetMedicalVolumeSegment(segMV, segT, segName="right femur") %[output:1adc1254]
%%
%[text] ### Plot the surface
%[text] **mmPlotMask2Surface** creates a surface from the mask and plots it. With the `transform` option, it transforms the vertices from voxels to mm.
rSeg.hp = mmPlotMask2Surface(rSeg.mask, ... %[output:group:04c43446] %[output:492edd75]
    fcolor=rSeg.color, ... % the Slicer color of the segment %[output:492edd75]
    falpha=0.75, ... %[output:492edd75]
    smooth=true, ... %[output:492edd75]
    transform=rSeg.tform, ... % transform the surface to mm %[output:492edd75]
    new_figure=true); %[output:group:04c43446] %[output:492edd75]
%[text] - Because we transformed the surface, the axes are in mm
%[text] - The output, ***hp***, is the handle of the patch. It holds the vertices and faces of the surface. If you close the figure, the patch is deleted, and you lose the vertices and faces. So keep the figure open until you have finished measuring \
%%
%[text] ## Align the femur
%[text] The femur is already close to vertical, but it isn't perfectly aligned with the z-axis, and that would make the range of its z-coordinates a little too short.
%[text] ### Center the surface at 0,0,0
%[text] We always center a surface before we rotate it. Otherwise, it rotates around the origin of the scanner, and swings away from where it was.
rSeg.hp.Vertices = rSeg.hp.Vertices - mean(rSeg.hp.Vertices); %[output:08af7c0d]
%%
%[text] ### Align the long axis with the x-axis
%[text] The course function **mmAlignSurface2Axes** finds the direction in which the vertices are most spread out, which for the femur is its long axis. It then rotates the vertices so that this direction lies along the x-axis.
rSeg.hp.Vertices = mmAlignSurface2Axes(rSeg.hp.Vertices); %[output:902dce49]
%[text] - The femur is now lying flat in the xy plane, like a femur on a bone box \
%%
%[text] ### Rotate the femur upright
%[text] To match the picture above, we rotate the femur 90º around the y-axis, so that its long axis lies along the z-axis. (This isn't strictly necessary; we could also measure along the x-axis.)
rSeg.hp.Vertices = mmRotateSurfaceVertices(rSeg.hp.Vertices, 'y', -90); %[output:5a5244b3]
%%
%[text] ## Measure the length
%[text] Now that the long axis of the femur lies along the z-axis, the length of the femur is the range of the z-coordinates:
rSeg.length = range(rSeg.hp.Vertices(:,3)) % mm %[output:93c61c6d]
%[text] - The length is already in mm, because we transformed the surface when we plotted it
%[text] - Compare this length with the one you measured in Slicer \
%%
%[text] ## Estimate height
%[text] Many studies have used linear regression to estimate height from femur length. This study gives one formula for males and one for females, with the femur length F1 in mm:
%[text] [https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1344622316300141](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1344622316300141)
%[text] ```
%[text] Male body height (cm) = (F1 * 2.38 + 614.1) / 10
%[text] Female body height (cm) = (F1 * 2.47 + 541.0) / 10
%[text] ```
heightCM = [(rSeg.length * 2.38 + 614.1)/10, (rSeg.length * 2.47 + 541.0)/10] % [male female], cm %[output:4ea1f264]
%[text] Convert to feet and inches:
heightIN = heightCM / 2.54; % inches
sexes = ["male" "female"];
for i = 1:2 %[output:group:3ff2daae]
    fprintf("If %s, estimated height is %d ft %.1f in\n", sexes(i), floor(heightIN(i)/12), mod(heightIN(i), 12)) %[output:0eaae23c]
end %[output:group:3ff2daae]
%[text] - The two estimates differ by about 3 cm. Which formula should we use? What else would you need to know about this person? \
%%
%[text] # Femur shaft diameter
%[text] How thick is the femur? The answer depends on where we measure: the femur is wide at the head and the condyles, and narrow along the shaft. It also depends on how we measure, because the shaft isn't a perfect cylinder.
%[text] Anthropologists measure the shaft at **midshaft**, halfway along the length of the bone, in two directions: front to back (anteroposterior, AP) and side to side (mediolateral, ML). In Slicer, we measured one of these, on one slice.
%[text] In MATLAB, we can measure the whole femur at once, with the course function **mmCrossSectionProfile**.
%%
%[text] ## How mmCrossSectionProfile works
%[text] **mmCrossSectionProfile** measures a structure along its length, the way you would measure a loaf of bread by slicing it:
%[text] 1. It turns every voxel into a point in mm, and finds the long axis of the points with **pca** (or, for a curved structure like the aorta, a smooth centerline)
%[text] 2. It cuts the structure into thin slabs perpendicular to that axis, two voxels thick
%[text] 3. It counts the voxels in each slab. Voxel count × voxel volume ÷ slab thickness = the cross-sectional area of the slab
%[text] 4. It converts each area to the **equivalent diameter**, the diameter of a circle with the same area. It also finds the **largest inscribed sphere** in each slab (with **bwdist**), and the narrowest and widest widths of each cross-section \
%[text] ![](text:image:c5a1)
%[text] The result is a **diameter profile** along the whole structure. The profile shows us where the shaft is, so we don't have to crop the femur first.
%[text] The function returns two outputs:
%[text] - a table with one row per slab, from the inferior end (the knee) to the superior end (the hip): the position of the slab (in mm and in % of the length), its area, its equivalent diameter, its largest inscribed sphere, and its narrowest and widest widths (**MinWidth\_mm** and **MaxWidth\_mm**)
%[text] - a structure with the length of the structure, and the cross-section at midshaft (**At**, 50% of the length by default) \
%%
%[text] ## Measure the right femur
[rProfile, rInfo] = mmCrossSectionProfile(rSeg, Title="Right femur"); %[output:04aaadd2]
%[text] - **Top left**: the femur, colored by the equivalent diameter of each slab. The black line is the long axis, and the white circle marks midshaft
%[text] - **Top right**: the cross-section at midshaft. The two colored lines show its narrowest and widest widths
%[text] - **Bottom**: the diameter profile. The two peaks are the **condyles** at the knee (0%) and the **head, neck, and greater trochanter** at the hip (100%). From about 30% to 70% of the length, the diameter is nearly constant: this is the **shaft** \
%[text] The table has one row per slab:
head(rProfile) %[output:24fa6965]
%[text] - top 8 rows of table \
%%
%[text] ### Midshaft
%[text] `rInfo.At` is the row of the table at midshaft:
rInfo.At %[output:30b0dda0]
%[text] - The equivalent diameter, about 26 mm, is the diameter of a circle with the same area as the cross-section
%[text] - The largest inscribed sphere is smaller (about 24 mm). The sphere has to fit inside the bone, so it touches the wall where the cross-section is narrowest
%[text] - The narrowest and widest widths (about 24 and 29 mm) show that the cross-section is a little oval \
%%
%[text] ### AP and ML widths
%[text] **MinWidth\_mm** and **MaxWidth\_mm** are the narrowest and widest widths in **any** direction. In the cross-section plot, they run at an angle, so they aren't the AP and ML widths that anthropologists measure.
%[text] The AP and ML widths are in `rInfo.CrossSection`, which measures the midshaft cross-section along the patient's own directions:
rInfo.CrossSection %[output:7535520e]
%[text] - **WidthLR\_mm** is the width from the patient's left to right. For the femur, that is the **mediolateral (ML)** width
%[text] - **WidthAP\_mm** is the width from front to back: the **anteroposterior (AP)** width
%[text] - **WidthSI\_mm** (superior-inferior) is NaN, because the femur runs up and down, so it has no up-down width at a cross-section
%[text] - The ML and AP widths always fall between the narrowest and widest widths
%[text] - Compare these widths with your Slicer measurement. Which direction did you measure in Slicer? \
%[text] (The function assumes that the femur is roughly upright, as it is in a CT of the legs. Then the patient's left-right direction is the bone's mediolateral direction.)
%%
%[text] ### Summary of the right femur
table(rSeg.length, rInfo.At.EquivDiameter_mm, rInfo.CrossSection.WidthLR_mm, rInfo.CrossSection.WidthAP_mm, rInfo.At.InscribedDiameter_mm, ... %[output:group:822dd5e6] %[output:45e6170a]
    VariableNames=["Length" "MidshaftEquivDiameter" "MidshaftML" "MidshaftAP" "MidshaftInscribed"]) %[output:group:822dd5e6] %[output:45e6170a]
%[text] - All values are in mm
%[text] - Which value would you report as "the diameter of the femur"? Why? \
%[text] A note on the inscribed sphere: another way to find it is to skeletonize the mask (**bwskel**) and read **bwdist** along the skeleton. But the skeleton of a thick bone doesn't always run through the exact center, and wherever it's off center the wall is closer. For this femur, that gives about 18 mm instead of 24 mm. **mmCrossSectionProfile** avoids the problem by taking the largest distance in each slab, wherever it is.
%%
%[text] # The left femur
%[text] How would we measure the left femur? Its condyles were replaced by the femoral part of the knee implant. To measure the full length of the bone, we combine the femur and the implant into one mask.
lSeg = mmGetMedicalVolumeSegment(segMV, segT, segName="left femur");
implantSeg = mmGetMedicalVolumeSegment(segMV, segT, segName="Implant");
lSeg.maskCombo = lSeg.mask | implantSeg.mask; % combine the femur and the implant
%%
lSeg.hp = mmPlotMask2Surface(lSeg.maskCombo, fcolor=lSeg.color, ... %[output:group:5a5be5d0] %[output:38b54b8c]
    falpha=1, ... %[output:38b54b8c]
    transform=lSeg.tform, ... % transform the surface to mm %[output:38b54b8c]
    new_figure=true); %[output:group:5a5be5d0] %[output:38b54b8c]
%%
lSeg.hp.Vertices = lSeg.hp.Vertices - mean(lSeg.hp.Vertices); % center at 0,0,0 %[output:5c54314a]
lSeg.hp.Vertices = mmAlignSurface2Axes(lSeg.hp.Vertices); % align the long axis with the x-axis %[output:5c54314a]
lSeg.hp.Vertices = mmRotateSurfaceVertices(lSeg.hp.Vertices, 'y', 90); % rotate upright %[output:5c54314a]
%[text] - We rotate this femur by +90º instead of -90º, because **mmAlignSurface2Axes** happened to point it the other way along the x-axis. Try -90º and see what happens \
%%
lSeg.length = range(lSeg.hp.Vertices(:,3)) % mm %[output:95de3d09]
lSeg.length - rSeg.length % difference between the left and right femur (mm) %[output:82e91b91]
%[text] - The two femurs are within about 2 mm of each other, as we would expect \
%%
%[text] ## Diameter profiles of both femurs
%[text] **mmCrossSectionProfile** also takes a mask, if we give it the transformation to mm. For the left femur, we use the mask with the implant included:
[lProfile, lInfo] = mmCrossSectionProfile(lSeg.maskCombo, Tform=lSeg.tform, Title="Left femur + implant"); %[output:219acaf9]
%%
%[text] The two tables have the same columns, so we can plot the two profiles together:
figure %[output:5ac37af4]
plot(rProfile.Percent, rProfile.EquivDiameter_mm, LineWidth=1.5, Color=rSeg.color) %[output:5ac37af4]
hold on %[output:5ac37af4]
plot(lProfile.Percent, lProfile.EquivDiameter_mm, LineWidth=1.5, Color=lSeg.color) %[output:5ac37af4]
xregion(30, 70, FaceColor=[0.5 0.5 0.5], FaceAlpha=0.15) % the shaft %[output:5ac37af4]
xline(50, "--", "midshaft") %[output:5ac37af4]
legend("right femur", "left femur + implant", "", "", Location="northeast") %[output:5ac37af4]
xlabel("Position along the femur (% of length, knee → hip)") %[output:5ac37af4]
ylabel("Equivalent diameter (mm)") %[output:5ac37af4]
xlim([0 100]) %[output:5ac37af4]
grid on %[output:5ac37af4]
%%
table(["right"; "left"], [rInfo.At.EquivDiameter_mm; lInfo.At.EquivDiameter_mm], ...
    [rInfo.CrossSection.WidthLR_mm; lInfo.CrossSection.WidthLR_mm], [rInfo.CrossSection.WidthAP_mm; lInfo.CrossSection.WidthAP_mm], ...
    VariableNames=["Femur" "MidshaftEquivDiameter" "MidshaftML" "MidshaftAP"])
%[text] - The shafts of the two femurs are similar: their equivalent diameters at midshaft are about 1 mm apart. But their shapes differ: at midshaft, the left femur is narrower from side to side (ML) than the right
%[text] - At the knee, the left profile includes the metal implant, which has a slightly larger cross-section than the condyles of the right femur \
%%
%[text] ## Questions
%[text] 1. How close was your femur length in MATLAB to your Slicer measurement? Which one do you trust more, and why?
%[text] 2. Why did we align the femur before we measured its length? What would happen if we measured the range of the z-coordinates without aligning it first?
%[text] 3. Your Slicer ruler measured one line on one slice. Which of the MATLAB measurements is it most similar to?
%[text] 4. Why is the largest inscribed sphere smaller than the equivalent diameter? When would the two agree?
%[text] 5. What would happen to the diameter profile if the femur were broken and badly healed? \
%%
%[text] # Challenge: the diameter of the aorta
%[text] In SLCR1 and SLCR2, you segmented the aorta in the CTA Cardio volume, and TotalSegmentator segmented it too. Use **mmCrossSectionProfile** to measure the aorta along its whole length.
%[text] Unlike the femur, the aorta is curved: it runs up from the abdomen, arches over the heart, and comes back down as the ascending aorta. Keep that in mind as you work through these steps.
%%
%[text] ### Step 1: Load the CTA Cardio segmentations
%[text] This code loads the example files. To use your own files, change `cta.project` to your own CTA Cardio folder, and the file names if you need to.
cta.project = fullfile(mmSetUnitDataFolder(3), "CTACardio"); % or the path to your own CTA Cardio folder
cta.segment = fullfile(cta.project, "Salcedo Segmentation.seg.nrrd"); % your segmentation
cta.total = fullfile(cta.project, "CTACardio Crop segmentation.seg.nrrd"); % TotalSegmentator segmentation

ctaSegMV = medicalVolume(cta.segment);
ctaTotalMV = mmMatchVolumeGeometry(medicalVolume(cta.total), ctaSegMV); % some versions of Slicer add an extra empty slice
ctaSegT = mmGetSlicerSegmentInfo(cta.segment);
ctaTotalT = mmGetSlicerSegmentInfo(cta.total);
%%
%[text] ### Step 2: Index out the aortas
%[text] Use **mmGetMedicalVolumeSegment** to index out the aorta from both segmentations (check the segment names in `ctaSegT` and `ctaTotalT`).
% your code here
%%
%[text] ### Step 3: Try a straight axis
%[text] Run **mmCrossSectionProfile** on the TotalSegmentator aorta, with the default straight axis.
%[text] - Look at the black line in the 3D view. Where does the straight axis go wrong?
%[text] - What happens to the profile, and to the widest width, at the arch? \
% your code here
%%
%[text] ### Step 4: Follow the curve
%[text] Run it again with `Centerline="curved"`. The function now traces a smooth centerline through the middle of the aorta, and cuts the slabs perpendicular to that line.
%[text] - How long is the aorta along the centerline? Compare it with the length from the straight axis
%[text] - Which length is right for a curved vessel? Which is right for the femur? \
% your code here
%%
%[text] ### Step 5: Measure your own aorta
%[text] Run it on your aorta, with `Centerline="curved"`.
%[text] - Look for bumps in the profile. Where are they in the 3D view? (Hint: what branches off the aorta?)
%[text] - Try removing the branch stumps first, with a morphological opening: `imopen(mask, strel("sphere", 5))`. Then run the function again. What changed? \
% your code here
%%
%[text] ### Step 6: Compare the two aortas
%[text] Plot the equivalent diameter of both aortas against `Position_mm`, in one figure.
%[text] - Along the descending aorta, which segmentation is wider? By how much? Why? (Hint: what did your threshold capture: the blood inside the aorta, or the whole vessel with its wall?)
%[text] - Why is the TotalSegmentator aorta longer? \
% your code here
%%
%[text] ### Step 7: Find the widest point
%[text] - Where along the aorta is it widest? Does that make sense anatomically?
%[text] - The normal aorta is about 2–3 cm wide at its root, and about 2 cm in the abdomen. An abdominal aorta wider than 3 cm is called an **aneurysm**. How do your measurements compare?
%[text] - Use the `At` option to get the cross-section at a position of your choice, and report its narrowest and widest widths. Is the aorta round there? \
% your code here
%%\

%[appendix]{"version":"1.0"}
%---
%[metadata:view]
%   data: {"layout":"inline"}
%---
%[control:filebrowser:5635]
%   data: {"browserType":"Folder","defaultValue":"\"\"","label":"Femur Folder","run":"Section"}
%---
%[control:spinner:4610]
%   data: {"defaultValue":1,"label":"Spinner","max":10,"min":1,"run":"Section","runOn":"ValueChanging","step":1}
%---
%[control:spinner:3201]
%   data: {"defaultValue":2,"label":"Spinner","max":10,"min":1,"run":"Section","runOn":"ValueChanging","step":1}
